## Proof

A fourth-rank tensor $T_{ijkl}$ is **isotropic** if, under every rotation $Q\in SO(3)$, its components transform back into themselves:

$$T'_{ijkl}=Q_{ip}Q_{jq}Q_{kr}Q_{ls}\,T_{pqrs}=T_{ijkl}.$$

We use the fundamental fact that the Kronecker delta $\delta_{ij}$ is an isotropic second-rank tensor. Indeed, for any orthogonal matrix $Q$ (so that $QQ^{T}=I$, i.e. $Q_{ip}Q_{jp}=\delta_{ij}$):

$$Q_{ip}Q_{jq}\,\delta_{pq}=Q_{ip}Q_{jp}=(QQ^{T})_{ij}=\delta_{ij}. \tag{$\star$}$$

We verify each tensor using only $(\star)$ and linearity of the transformation rule.

---

### (a) $A_{ijkl}=\delta_{ij}\delta_{kl}$

$$\begin{aligned}
A'_{ijkl}&=Q_{ip}Q_{jq}Q_{kr}Q_{ls}\,\delta_{pq}\delta_{rs}\\
&=\bigl(Q_{ip}Q_{jq}\,\delta_{pq}\bigr)\bigl(Q_{kr}Q_{ls}\,\delta_{rs}\bigr)\\
&=\delta_{ij}\,\delta_{kl}\qquad\text{(by $(\star)$ applied twice)}\\
&=A_{ijkl}.
\end{aligned}$$

So $A$ is isotropic. $\checkmark$

---

### (b) $B_{ijkl}=\delta_{ik}\delta_{jl}+\delta_{il}\delta_{jk}$

By linearity, $B'=B'_{(1)}+B'_{(2)}$, where $B_{(1)}=\delta_{ik}\delta_{jl}$ and $B_{(2)}=\delta_{il}\delta_{jk}$.

For the first term:
$$\begin{aligned}
(B_{(1)})'_{ijkl}&=Q_{ip}Q_{jq}Q_{kr}Q_{ls}\,\delta_{pr}\delta_{qs}\\
&=\bigl(Q_{ip}Q_{kr}\,\delta_{pr}\bigr)\bigl(Q_{jq}Q_{ls}\,\delta_{qs}\bigr)\\
&=(QQ^{T})_{ik}\,(QQ^{T})_{jl}\\
&=\delta_{ik}\,\delta_{jl}\qquad\text{(by $(\star)$).}
\end{aligned}$$

For the second term:
$$\begin{aligned}
(B_{(2)})'_{ijkl}&=Q_{ip}Q_{jq}Q_{kr}Q_{ls}\,\delta_{ps}\delta_{qr}\\
&=\bigl(Q_{ip}Q_{ls}\,\delta_{ps}\bigr)\bigl(Q_{jq}Q_{kr}\,\delta_{qr}\bigr)\\
&=(QQ^{T})_{il}\,(QQ^{T})_{jk}\\
&=\delta_{il}\,\delta_{jk}\qquad\text{(by $(\star)$).}
\end{aligned}$$

Adding:
$$B'_{ijkl}=\delta_{ik}\delta_{jl}+\delta_{il}\delta_{jk}=B_{ijkl}.$$

So $B$ is isotropic. $\checkmark$

---

### (c) $C_{ijkl}=\delta_{ik}\delta_{jl}-\delta_{il}\delta_{jk}$

The computation is identical to part (b) except for the minus sign. By linearity,
$$C'_{ijkl}=(B_{(1)})'_{ijkl}-(B_{(2)})'_{ijkl}=\delta_{ik}\delta_{jl}-\delta_{il}\delta_{jk}=C_{ijkl}.$$

So $C$ is isotropic. $\checkmark$

---

### Remark on the class of transformations

The argument used only the orthogonality relation $QQ^{T}=I$, so the conclusion actually holds for **all** orthogonal transformations $Q\in O(3)$ (rotations *and* reflections), not merely proper rotations $SO(3)$. In particular it holds for every rotation, which is the requirement of the problem.

---

### Summary

All three tensors are built from the isotropic second-rank tensor $\delta_{ij}$ via products and linear combinations. Since the transformation rule is multilinear and since each factor $\delta$ returns to itself under any orthogonal change of basis by $(\star)$, each of $A$, $B$, $C$ is invariant:

$$A'_{ijkl}=A_{ijkl},\qquad B'_{ijkl}=B_{ijkl},\qquad C'_{ijkl}=C_{ijkl}.$$

Hence each is an isotropic fourth-rank tensor. $\blacksquare$

